// @test: run
// @tier: 1
// @level: L0
// @tags: vbc, aot, heap, returned-reference
// @timeout: 180000
// @expected-exit: 0
// @expected-stdout-begin
// star 41
// deref 41
// as_ref 41
// into_inner 13
// @expected-stdout-end

// `Heap<T>` IS the inner value at Tier 1 — `Heap.new(x)` passes `x`
// through and adds no indirection, which is why `*h` has always
// answered. The accessors did not: `h.as_ref()` and `h.into_inner()`
// both SIGSEGV'd while `*h` printed 41 on the same binary, one
// programme per line.
//
// The emitted IR said why in three lines:
//
//     define internal fastcc i64 @Heap.as_ref()    <- no self parameter
//       store i64 41, ptr %r0_slot                 <- the transparent VALUE
//       %p = inttoptr i64 41 to ptr
//       getelementptr i8, ptr %p, i64 24; load     <- reads address 65
//
// `Heap.new` had already been folded away, so the callee's receiver WAS
// the value 41, and `core/base/memory.vr`'s `as_ref` — `self.ptr` on a
// 3-field record — read 24 bytes past it. The same shape as the
// `into_raw`/`from_raw`/`is_valid` intercepts that already existed
// beside it, whose own comment names the failure mode verbatim:
// "misreads the transparent value as a 3-field record".
//
// The MUTABLE family is deliberately not here. `as_mut`, `deref_mut`
// and `leak` return `&mut T`, and under transparency there is no
// storage for a write to reach — an identity would hand back a copy.
// Making them identity turned a SIGTRAP into a printed heap address,
// which is the one direction this must not move; they keep the
// behaviour they had, and Tier 0 refuses them with a diagnostic that
// names the carrier substitution.
//
// `*h` is in this file because it is the reading that already worked:
// a fix that traded it would be no fix.

fn main() {
    let h = Heap.new(41);
    print(f"star {*h}");
    print(f"deref {h.deref()}");
    print(f"as_ref {*h.as_ref()}");
    let w = Heap.new(13);
    print(f"into_inner {w.into_inner()}");
}
